    <statement><p>Use Green's Theorem to evaluate the
line integral of <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\lt  x^{4}, 6 x\right&gt;</m></p>
<p>around the
boundary of the parallelogram in the following figure 
(note the orientation).</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__17_Fundamental_Theorems_of_Vector_Analysis__17__1_Greens_Theorem__17__1__9__pg-image-1.png" width="39%"/>
<p>With <m>x_0=5</m> and <m>y_0=5</m>.</p>
<p><m>\int_{\mathcal{C}} x^{4} \,dx+6 x \,dy =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-150</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>P= x^{4}</m> and <m>Q=6 x</m>, therefore
<me>\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}=6-0=6</me>
Hence, Green's Theorem implies
<me>\int_{\partial\mathcal{D}} x^{4} \,dx+6 x \,dy =
\iint_{\mathcal{D}} \left(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}\right) \,dA =</me><me>\iint_{\mathcal{D}} 6 \,dA=6 \iint_{\mathcal{D}}  \,dA
= 6\, \mathrm{Area}(\mathcal{D})=6 \cdot 25=150</me>
So now accounting for the orientation,
<me>\int_{\mathcal{C}} x^{4} \,dx+6 x \,dy =
-\int_{\partial\mathcal{D}} x^{4} \,dx+6 x \,dy =-150</me>



</solution>
